// Recorded discovery-oracle output, so the parsers are testable with no harness
// installed.
//
// This module is NOT a test. It lives beside `git-fixture.ts` and
// `termination-oracle.ts` for the reason their headers give: `run-tests.mjs`
// reads `dist/test` without recursion and keeps only `entry.isFile()`, so
// `dist/test/helpers` is never enumerated as a suite. It is a `.ts` module and
// not a `.json` file because only TypeScript under `test/` is compiled into
// `dist/test` at all.
//
// PROVENANCE. Every constant below is the stdout of one live run made while
// executing plan 03-04, on Windows 11 with claude 2.1.267, codex 0.152.0 and pi
// 0.85.1. The probe project held four skills — `zzz-canary-widget` and
// `scientific-thinking-literature-review` under both `.agents/skills` and
// `.claude/skills`, `zzz-codexlocal-widget` under `.codex/skills`, and
// `zzz-pi-widget` under `.pi/skills`. The control directory was an empty
// sibling of the project, so no ancestor of it held a project skill root.
//
// SUBSTITUTIONS. The recordings are verbatim except for exactly these, applied
// by the generator and enumerated here so a reader can tell what was changed
// from what a harness said:
//
//  1. Host roots are aliased to synthetic ABSOLUTE roots, in all three
//     spellings a recording uses (backslash, forward slash, JSON-escaped
//     backslash): the probe project to `/synthetic/project`, the control
//     directory to `/synthetic/control`, their shared parent to
//     `/synthetic/probe`, and the developer home to `/synthetic/home`. A
//     recording must not carry a home directory into the repository (T-03-32),
//     and the synthetic roots stay absolute so the "resolve the label to an
//     absolute path" assertion is still about an absolute path.
//  2. In the codex recordings, the two content items that are neither the
//     skills block nor the environment context — a sandbox-permissions
//     instruction and a plugin recommendation list — have their bodies replaced
//     with an elision marker. They are third-party prompt prose, not ours to
//     redistribute, and no assertion reads them. The message array's shape, its
//     roles, and every other item are untouched.
//  3. In the claude recordings, session and hook identifiers are replaced with
//     fixed synthetic values so the fixture is deterministic, and the
//     developer's personal cloud-connector MCP servers are renamed
//     `connector-*`. Their COUNT, ORDER and STATUS are preserved, because those
//     are what the parser reads; only the names are personal.
//  4. In the pi recordings, the extension UI request's random id is replaced
//     with a fixed one.
//
// WHAT THE RECORDINGS SHOW, and why each is kept:
//
//  - codex inside vs outside: the root labels are POSITIONAL and shift. `r0` is
//    the project's own `.codex/skills` inside and the user's `~/.agents/skills`
//    outside, so a ledger keyed on a label is keyed on the wrong thing.
//  - codex and pi both advertise `literature-review`; claude advertises
//    `scientific-thinking-literature-review`. Same file, same session, three
//    harnesses, two answers (03-RESEARCH.md Pitfall 2).
//  - pi publishes its own `project` / `user` scope discriminator, nested inside
//    the source-info object rather than in the flat field its shipped
//    documentation describes.
//  - pi outside and pi with trust withheld both list zero project-scope skills:
//    two independent negatives for one positive.
//  - the claude init event is NOT the first line of the stream. Hook events
//    precede it on a host with session hooks configured, so it is found by its
//    type and subtype.
//  - claude's MCP servers carry mixed statuses in the same event — `pending`,
//    `connected`, `needs-auth` — which is why status is recorded as a
//    registration fact rather than judged.

